#lang ivy1.7

# ############################################################################
#
#   TOMASULO'S ALGORITHM -- a documented Ivy model and its safety proof
#
#   This file is a heavily annotated rewrite of `tomasulo.ivy`, which is an
#   Ivy encoding of the out-of-order execution engine of a CPU (Tomasulo's
#   algorithm, IBM 360/91, 1967).  `tomasulo.ivy` carries
#
#       attribute method = vmt
#
#   which means that `ivy_check` does not verify it at all: it *transliterates*
#   it into `tomasulo.vmt` for an external model checker.  That is not an
#   accident -- as shipped, the model is outside Ivy's decidable fragment and
#   its invariants are all commented out (the two that are left enabled fail).
#   See section 3 below: the fix is a handful of "obscure" encoding moves whose
#   only purpose is to keep the verification conditions in EPR.  With those
#   moves the whole refinement proof is discharged by `ivy_check` in under a
#   second, with no auxiliary lemmas and no interaction:
#
#       $ ~/workplace/venv_ivy/bin/ivy_check tomasulo_documented.ivy
#       ...
#       OK
#
#   Companion file: `tomasulo_liveness.ivy` states and proves a *liveness*
#   property of the same machine ("no instruction starves in a reservation
#   station") using McMillan's relational-ranking rules; see LIVENESS_GUIDE.md.
#
# ############################################################################
#
#   1.  WHAT TOMASULO'S ALGORITHM IS
#   ================================
#
#   A simple CPU executes instructions strictly in order: fetch, read the
#   register operands, compute, write the destination register, repeat.  That
#   is slow, because an instruction whose operand is not ready yet blocks every
#   instruction behind it, even the ones that could have run immediately.
#
#   Tomasulo's algorithm lets instructions execute *out of order* while
#   preserving the results of the in-order machine.  It has four ingredients:
#
#   (a) RESERVATION STATIONS.  Instead of waiting in a queue, a decoded
#       instruction is parked in a buffer entry called a reservation station
#       (RS).  Each station holds the opcode, the two operand slots, and a
#       destination.  An operand slot is in one of two states:
#
#           valid            -- it already holds the operand *value*
#           not valid        -- it holds the *tag* of the station that is
#                               going to produce the value
#
#   (b) REGISTER RENAMING VIA TAGS.  Every reservation station has a unique
#       name, its "tag".  When an instruction is placed in station t with
#       destination register r, the machine marks r as *reserved with tag t*.
#       From then on, any later instruction that reads r does not read a value
#       -- it copies the tag t into its operand slot and waits.  The tag is
#       therefore a *forward reference* to a value that does not exist yet.
#       This is what removes write-after-read and write-after-write hazards:
#       the architectural register r no longer has one meaning, it has one
#       meaning per in-flight writer, and the tag distinguishes them.
#
#   (c) THE COMMON DATA BUS (CDB).  When an execution unit finishes, it puts
#       the pair (tag, value) on a single broadcast bus.  Every register and
#       every operand slot that is waiting for that tag latches the value in
#       the same cycle ("snooping").  This is the *wake-up* mechanism, and it
#       is the only way a not-valid operand slot ever becomes valid.  Exactly
#       one result per cycle can be broadcast, so the CDB is the machine's
#       central arbitrated resource.
#
#   (d) EXECUTION UNITS.  A station whose two operands are both valid may be
#       *issued* to any free execution unit (EU), which computes the result
#       after an unpredictable number of cycles and then asks for the CDB.
#       Because issue only depends on operand readiness, instructions leave
#       the stations in dataflow order, not program order.
#
#   The life of one ALU instruction, in this model, is therefore:
#
#       fetch/dispatch      -> station t becomes valid, operands captured
#                              (as values, or as tags of earlier stations)
#       wake-up (0..n times)-> CDB broadcasts the tags t is waiting for
#       issue               -> t.issued := true, some EU takes the operands
#       execute             -> the EU becomes "ready" (result computed)
#       complete/writeback  -> the EU wins the CDB; the value is broadcast;
#                              station t is freed; registers and stations
#                              waiting on tag t latch the value
#
# ############################################################################
#
#   2.  WHAT IS BEING PROVED (THE CORRECTNESS STATEMENT)
#   ====================================================
#
#   Tomasulo is not interesting because it satisfies some ad-hoc invariant; it
#   is interesting because it is *equivalent to the in-order machine*.  So the
#   property proved here is a REFINEMENT:
#
#       the out-of-order machine `toma` implements the in-order machine `ref`.
#
#   `ref` is a three-line specification CPU with a register file `ref.r` that
#   executes each accepted instruction atomically and in order (object `ref`,
#   below).  The two machines are run in lockstep: every non-stalling step of
#   `toma` also steps `ref` (the call `ref.step(...)` at the end of
#   `toma.step`; `ref` is ghost -- it exists only in the proof).
#
#   The refinement is stated by two facts, both invariants below:
#
#       [inv_reg_ready]     ~ir(R).resvd -> ir(R).val = ref.r(R)
#
#           an architectural register that is not reserved holds exactly the
#           value the in-order machine has in it.
#
#       [inv_reg_reserved]  ir(R).resvd & ir(R).tag(K) -> st(K).valid
#                                & aux(K).res = ref.r(R) & st(K).dst = R
#
#           a reserved register is owned by a live station K, and the value
#           that K will eventually broadcast is *already* the value the
#           in-order machine has in R.  In other words the renaming is a
#           faithful promise.
#
#   Together they give the observable statement, which is asserted directly in
#   the code as [refines_read]:
#
#           a register read that does not stall returns the in-order value.
#
#   THE GHOST ("aux") STATE.  The proof needs to talk about "the value station
#   K will produce" before K has produced it.  That is what the `aux` array
#   is: a ghost record per tag, written by `ref.step` at dispatch time, that
#   records the operand values and the result the *in-order* machine computed
#   for the instruction now sitting in station K.  The invariants then say
#   that whatever the hardware eventually computes for K agrees with `aux(K)`.
#   `aux` is a classic history/prophecy variable: it makes a statement about
#   the future ("K will produce this value") checkable one step at a time.
#
# ############################################################################
#
#   3.  THE "OBSCURE" PART: KEEPING THE VERIFICATION CONDITIONS IN EPR
#   ==================================================================
#
#   Ivy only promises decidable, counterexample-producing verification when
#   the verification conditions land in EPR (effectively propositional logic:
#   prenex forall*-exists* over an uninterpreted signature, with an acyclic
#   quantifier-alternation/function graph).  The relevant rule of thumb is:
#
#       a FUNCTION whose result sort can be fed back into its own argument
#       position, under a universal quantifier, creates a cycle and can
#       generate an infinite sequence of instantiations.
#
#   `tomasulo.ivy` has two such functions, and both show up in the invariants
#   that the refinement needs:
#
#     (i)  the operand tag,  st(K).opra.tag : tag_t -> tag_t.
#          The invariant that makes the proof work is
#
#              st(K).valid & ~st(K).opra.valid & st(K).opra.tag = T
#                  -> st(T).valid & aux(T).res = aux(K).opra
#
#          ("if K is waiting for T, then T is live and will produce exactly
#          the operand K is waiting for").  With K universally quantified, the
#          term `st(K).opra.tag` is itself a tag and can be substituted for K,
#          giving opra.tag(opra.tag(opra.tag(...))) forever.  Running
#          `ivy_check` on `tomasulo.ivy` with these invariants enabled prints
#
#              error: The verification condition is not in the fragment FAU.
#              The following terms may generate an infinite sequence of
#              instantiations:  toma.st.oprb.tag(K_d) = T_b
#
#     (ii) the ALU,  f : word_t * word_t -> word_t, likewise cyclic in word_t
#          (f(f(x,y),z) ...).
#
#   THE FIX, in both cases, is to replace the function by a RELATION:
#
#          var      tag : tag_t                ~~>  relation tag(T:tag_t)
#          function f(X,Y) : word_t            ~~>  relation f(X,Y,Z)
#                                                   + a functionality axiom
#
#   A relation applied to universally quantified variables produces no new
#   terms, so the cycle disappears and every VC is a decidable EPR query.  The
#   price is that we must now *say* what the function symbol used to say for
#   free: the ALU gets `axiom f(X,Y,Z1) & f(X,Y,Z2) -> Z1 = Z2`, and the
#   reference machine computes a result by `res := *; assume f(opra,oprb,res)`
#   instead of `res := f(opra,oprb)`.  Note that we do NOT state totality
#   (`exists Z. f(X,Y,Z)`): that is a forall-exists formula and would
#   reintroduce a Skolem function word_t*word_t -> word_t, i.e. the very cycle
#   we removed.  Totality is instead a meta-level assumption; it costs nothing
#   in soundness (an `assume` can only remove behaviours) but it does mean the
#   model should be checked for vacuity separately -- see section 8.
#
#   Nothing else about the tag relations is constrained.  In particular we do
#   not axiomatise "every station has exactly one operand tag".  We do not
#   need to: the code only ever assigns tag relations from other tag relations
#   or from a singleton (`ir(dst).tag(X) := st_choice = X`), and the
#   invariants below only ever read them in the *positive* position
#   `... .tag(T) -> ...`, which is exactly the EPR-friendly shape.
#
#   A third, smaller encoding move: `pout.tag`.  `pout` is a single record
#   (the CDB), not an array, so `pout.tag : tag_t` is a CONSTANT, not a
#   function of tag_t -- constants create no cycle.  So the bus tag stays a
#   plain variable and is *related* to the EU tag relation by an `assume`
#   (line marked [cdb_tag]).
#
# ############################################################################
#
#   4.  READING THE MODEL
#   =====================
#
#   The whole machine is ONE atomic action, `toma.step`, which models one
#   hardware clock cycle of a synchronous design.  Everything that a real
#   pipeline does in parallel in a cycle happens inside that action, in the
#   order in which the combinational logic settles:
#
#       phase 1   CDB arbitration + completion       (reads eu, writes pout)
#       phase 2   stall computation                  (reads st, ir)
#       phase 3   writeback/wake-up from the CDB     (writes ir, st)
#       phase 4   dispatch of the incoming op        (writes st, ir)
#       phase 5   issue station -> execution unit    (writes st, eu)
#       phase 6   start execution                    (writes eu)
#       phase 7   ghost: step the reference machine
#
#   The *inputs* of the cycle are the parameters of `toma.step`.  They are
#   universally quantified by `export`, i.e. chosen adversarially each cycle:
#
#       opin, srca, srcb, dst, din   the instruction arriving from decode
#       st_choice                    which station the dispatcher allocates
#       issue_choice                 which station the issue arbiter picks
#       issue_eu                     which execution unit it is issued to
#       complete_eu                  which execution unit wins the CDB
#
#   Modelling an arbiter as an adversarial input is the standard trick for
#   *safety*: it proves correctness for every possible arbitration policy.  It
#   is also exactly what makes the model useless for *liveness* -- an
#   adversarial arbiter starves everything -- which is why the companion
#   liveness file has to add fairness assumptions on these choices.
#
#   One more input is hidden: `eu(E).ready` is declared but never assigned.
#   In Ivy an unassigned state variable is an oracle whose value is fixed at
#   initialisation, so here it models "this EU's result is available".  Safety
#   holds for every choice of `ready`; for liveness it must become dynamic
#   (an EU that is busy must eventually become ready), and that is one of the
#   changes made in `tomasulo_liveness.ivy`.
#
# ############################################################################


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4.1  Sorts
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# All four sorts are uninterpreted: the proof holds for any word width, any
# number of architectural registers, any number of reservation stations, and
# any number of execution units.  This parameterisation is free in first-order
# logic and is the main reason to verify hardware this way rather than by
# model checking a fixed-size instance.

type word_t                        # machine words (ALU operands and results)
type reg_t                         # architectural register names
type tag_t                         # reservation-station names == renaming tags
type eu_t                          # execution units

# The instruction set.  op_alu is the only interesting one: it is the only
# opcode that allocates a station, reserves a register, and needs renaming.
#   op_rd  reads a register out of the machine (the only observable output)
#   op_wr  writes an immediate into a register
#   op_nop does nothing (lets the environment idle a cycle)
type opcode = {op_alu,op_rd,op_wr,op_nop}


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4.2  An operand slot of a reservation station
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `valid` distinguishes the two states of section 1(a).  When valid, `val` is
# the operand value and `tag` is meaningless; when not valid, `tag` names the
# station that will produce the value and `val` is meaningless.
#
# `tag` is a RELATION, not a `var tag : tag_t` -- this is encoding move (i) of
# section 3.  Read `tag(T)` as "this slot is (also) waiting for T"; in every
# reachable state the code keeps it a singleton, but we never have to say so.

module st_opr_r = {
    var valid : bool
    relation tag(T:tag_t)
    var val : word_t
}

# The CDB record.  `pout` is a single instance, so its `tag` may stay a
# scalar: a constant symbol is not a function and creates no EPR cycle.
module st_opr = {
    var valid : bool
    var tag : tag_t
    var val : word_t
}


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4.3  The ALU
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# An uninterpreted binary operation over words: the proof is independent of
# what the ALU actually computes, it only needs the two machines to compute
# *the same* thing.  Encoded as a relation + functionality axiom, per section
# 3 (ii).

relation f(X:word_t,Y:word_t,Z:word_t)
axiom [alu_is_a_function] f(X,Y,Z1) & f(X,Y,Z2) -> Z1 = Z2


# ############################################################################
#  5.  THE REFERENCE MACHINE (specification, ghost)
# ############################################################################
#
# `ref` is the in-order CPU that `toma` must implement.  It has nothing but a
# register file, and each step reads its operands, computes, and writes back,
# atomically.  This is the definition of "what the program means".
#
# `ref.step` also writes the ghost `aux` array; that assignment logically
# belongs to `toma` (hence the comment kept from the original file), but it is
# convenient to put it here because this is the only place where the in-order
# operand and result values exist as program variables.

object ref = {

    action step(opin : opcode,
                srca:reg_t, srcb:reg_t, dst:reg_t,
                din: word_t,
                st_choice : tag_t,            # only used to index the ghost aux
                issue_choice : tag_t)
    returns (dout : word_t)

    var r(R:reg_t) : word_t                   # the architectural register file

    implement step {
        var opra : word_t;
        var oprb : word_t;
        var res : word_t;

        if opin = op_alu {
            opra := r(srca);
            oprb := r(srcb);
            res := *;                         # see section 3 (ii): the ALU is
            assume f(opra,oprb,res);          # a relation, so pick + constrain
            r(dst) := res;
        }
        else if opin = op_rd {
            dout := r(srca);
        }
        else if opin = op_wr {
            r(dst) := din;
        };

        # ---- ghost bookkeeping ------------------------------------------
        # Record, for the station that is being allocated in this very cycle,
        # the in-order operand values and the in-order result.  Everything the
        # out-of-order machine does with tag `st_choice` from now on is
        # checked against this record.
        if(opin = op_alu){
            toma.aux(st_choice).opra := opra;
            toma.aux(st_choice).oprb := oprb;
            toma.aux(st_choice).res  := res;
            toma.aux(st_choice).srca := srca;
            toma.aux(st_choice).srcb := srcb;
        };
    }
}


# ############################################################################
#  6.  THE OUT-OF-ORDER MACHINE
# ############################################################################

object toma = {

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # 6.1  Architectural register file, with renaming information.
    #
    #   resvd   -- "the architectural value of this register is not here;
    #              an in-flight instruction owns it"
    #   tag     -- which station owns it (relation; see section 3)
    #   val     -- the value, meaningful only when ~resvd
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    object ir(self:reg_t) = {
        var resvd : bool
        relation tag(T:tag_t)
        var val : word_t
    }

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # 6.2  The reservation stations, indexed by tag.
    #
    #   valid   -- this station holds an instruction that has not retired
    #   opra    -- operand slot A (value or tag)
    #   oprb    -- operand slot B
    #   dst     -- destination register.  Ghost: the RTL does not need it,
    #              but the proof does (invariant [inv_reg_reserved]).
    #   issued  -- this station's operands have been handed to an EU.  A
    #              station is freed only when a result with its own tag comes
    #              back on the CDB, and only if it was issued -- `issued` is
    #              what prevents a station from being freed by a *stale*
    #              broadcast of a previous tenant of the same tag.
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    object st(self:tag_t) = {
        var valid : bool
        instance opra : st_opr_r
        instance oprb : st_opr_r
        var dst : reg_t
        var issued : bool
    }

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # 6.3  Execution units.
    #
    #   valid   -- occupied by an operation
    #   ready   -- its result has been computed (an oracle here: see 4)
    #   res     -- the result
    #   tag     -- whose result it is (relation)
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    object eu(self:eu_t) = {
        var valid : bool
        var ready : bool
        var res : word_t
        relation tag(T:tag_t)
    }

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # 6.4  The ghost record, one per tag.  Written by ref.step at dispatch.
    #
    #   opra,oprb,res  what the in-order machine read and computed for the
    #                  instruction now in this station
    #   srca,srcb      its source registers (kept for readability; not used
    #                  by the invariants below)
    #   eu             which execution unit the station was issued to
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    object aux(self:tag_t) = {
        var opra : word_t
        var oprb : word_t
        var res : word_t
        var srca : reg_t
        var srcb : reg_t
        var eu : eu_t
    }

    # The common data bus.
    instance pout : st_opr

    after init {
        st(T).valid := false;              # no instruction in flight
        ir(R).resvd := false;              # no register renamed
        eu(E).valid := false;              # no execution unit busy

        # The EU tag relations are unconstrained at init, and `complete_eu`'s
        # tag relation is read through an `assume` in phase 1.  If it happened
        # to be empty, that `assume` would be unsatisfiable and *every* step
        # would be blocked -- the model would be vacuously correct.  Starting
        # it full costs nothing (no invariant mentions eu.tag while ~eu.valid)
        # and removes the hole.
        eu(E).tag(T) := true;

        # The two machines start in the same state.  (ir(R).val is arbitrary;
        # we simply define ref's register file to agree with it.)
        ref.r(R) := ir(R).val;
    }


    # ====================================================================
    # 6.5  ONE CLOCK CYCLE
    # ====================================================================
    action step (
      opin : opcode,
      srca:reg_t, srcb:reg_t, dst:reg_t,
      din: word_t,
      st_choice : tag_t,                   # dispatcher's choice of station
      issue_choice : tag_t,                # issue arbiter's choice of station
      issue_eu : eu_t,                     # issue arbiter's choice of EU
      complete_eu : eu_t                   # CDB arbiter's choice of EU
    )
    returns (
      stallout : bool,
      dout : word_t
    )
    = {
        var exe_rdy : bool;
        var exe_valid : bool;
        var exe_tag : tag_t;
        var exe_opra : word_t;
        var exe_oprb : word_t;

        # ----------------------------------------------------------------
        # PHASE 1 -- CDB arbitration and completion.
        #
        # The arbiter offers the bus to `complete_eu`.  The bus carries a
        # result iff that unit is occupied and has finished.  The unit is
        # released in the same cycle.
        # ----------------------------------------------------------------
        pout.valid := eu(complete_eu).valid & eu(complete_eu).ready;
        pout.val := eu(complete_eu).res;
        pout.tag := *;                                      # [cdb_tag]
        assume eu(complete_eu).tag(pout.tag);               # (section 3, end)
        if pout.valid {
            eu(complete_eu).valid := false
        };

        # ----------------------------------------------------------------
        # PHASE 2 -- structural and data hazards on the incoming instruction.
        #
        #   an ALU op stalls if the station the dispatcher picked is busy
        #     (structural hazard -- the only backpressure in the machine);
        #   a read stalls if the register it reads is renamed
        #     (a read is the machine's observable output, so it must not be
        #      answered with a stale value; this is precisely the condition
        #      under which [inv_reg_ready] licenses the answer).
        # A write never stalls: it *overwrites* the renaming, which is legal
        # because from the in-order machine's point of view it is the newest
        # writer of that register.
        # ----------------------------------------------------------------
        stallout := opin = op_alu & st(st_choice).valid
                    | opin = op_rd & ir(srca).resvd;

        # ----------------------------------------------------------------
        # PHASE 3 -- writeback / wake-up.
        #
        # Everything snoops the bus in the same cycle:
        #   * a register reserved with the broadcast tag latches the value
        #     and stops being reserved (the renaming is redeemed);
        #   * every operand slot waiting for the broadcast tag latches the
        #     value and becomes valid;
        #   * the producing station itself is freed (`issued` guards against
        #     freeing a station that merely reuses the tag, see 6.2).
        # Note these are simultaneous assignments over ALL registers R and ALL
        # tags T -- one line of Ivy for a whole comparator array in the RTL.
        # ----------------------------------------------------------------
        if(pout.valid){
            ir(R).val := (pout.val) if (ir(R).resvd & ir(R).tag(pout.tag)) else (ir(R).val);
            ir(R).resvd := ~ir(R).tag(pout.tag) & ir(R).resvd;

            st(T).opra.val := (pout.val) if ~st(T).opra.valid & st(T).opra.tag(pout.tag) else st(T).opra.val;
            st(T).opra.valid := st(T).opra.tag(pout.tag) | st(T).opra.valid;

            st(T).oprb.val := (pout.val) if ~st(T).oprb.valid & st(T).oprb.tag(pout.tag) else st(T).oprb.val;
            st(T).oprb.valid := st(T).oprb.tag(pout.tag) | st(T).oprb.valid;

            st(T).valid := ~(st(T).issued & pout.tag = T) & st(T).valid;
        };

        # ----------------------------------------------------------------
        # PHASE 4 -- dispatch the incoming instruction.
        #
        # For an ALU op: allocate station `st_choice`, capture both operands,
        # and rename `dst` to `st_choice`.
        #
        # Each operand is captured with a BYPASS: the bus broadcast of phase 3
        # and the register read of phase 4 happen in the same cycle, so if the
        # register we are reading is reserved with exactly the tag that is on
        # the bus right now, we take the bus value directly.  (In phase-3
        # terms the register has already been updated, but the code reads the
        # *pre-phase-3* ir because Ivy's parallel assignment in phase 3 wrote
        # ir and we re-derive here; the bypass is the reason the guards
        # mention `pout` at all.)  Without the bypass the instruction would
        # capture the tag of a station that is retiring this very cycle and
        # would then wait for a broadcast that never comes again -- a deadlock
        # that the liveness proof in the companion file would catch.
        # ----------------------------------------------------------------
        if(~stallout) {
            if opin = op_alu {

                st(st_choice).valid := true;
                st(st_choice).issued := false;

                # operand A, with bypass
                if(pout.valid & ir(srca).resvd & ir(srca).tag(pout.tag) ) {
                    st(st_choice).opra.valid := true;
                    st(st_choice).opra.tag(X) := ir(srca).tag(X);
                    st(st_choice).opra.val := pout.val;
                } else {
                    st(st_choice).opra.valid := ~ir(srca).resvd;
                    st(st_choice).opra.tag(X) := ir(srca).tag(X);
                    st(st_choice).opra.val := ir(srca).val;
                };

                # operand B, with bypass
                if(pout.valid & ir(srcb).resvd & ir(srcb).tag(pout.tag)){
                    st(st_choice).oprb.valid := true;
                    st(st_choice).oprb.tag(X) := ir(srcb).tag(X);
                    st(st_choice).oprb.val := pout.val;
                } else {
                    st(st_choice).oprb.valid := ~ir(srcb).resvd;
                    st(st_choice).oprb.tag(X) := ir(srcb).tag(X);
                    st(st_choice).oprb.val := ir(srcb).val;
                };

                # rename the destination register to this station
                ir(dst).resvd := true;
                ir(dst).tag(X) := st_choice = X;

                st(st_choice).dst := dst            # ghost, for the proof
            }
            else if opin = op_rd {
                # The observable output.  ~stallout guarantees ~ir(srca).resvd,
                # so [inv_reg_ready] says this is the in-order value; that is
                # checked explicitly in phase 7.
                dout := ir(srca).val;
            }
            else if opin = op_wr {
                ir(dst).val := din;
                ir(dst).resvd := false;             # cancels any renaming
            }
        };

        # ----------------------------------------------------------------
        # PHASE 5 -- issue: hand a ready station to a free execution unit.
        #
        # The station chosen by the arbiter fires iff it is live, both of its
        # operands are valid, it has not already been issued, and the chosen
        # unit is free.  Note this is the *only* place `issued` is set, and it
        # is set at most once per tenancy of a station.
        # ----------------------------------------------------------------
        exe_rdy := ~eu(issue_eu).valid;

        if(st(issue_choice).valid
        & st(issue_choice).opra.valid
        & st(issue_choice).oprb.valid
        & ~st(issue_choice).issued
        & exe_rdy)
        {
            exe_valid := true;
            st(issue_choice).issued := true;
        }
        else exe_valid := false;

        exe_tag  := issue_choice;
        exe_opra := st(issue_choice).opra.val;
        exe_oprb := st(issue_choice).oprb.val;

        # ----------------------------------------------------------------
        # PHASE 6 -- start execution.
        #
        # The unit latches the operands and computes.  `ready` is not written
        # here: how long the computation takes is an oracle (section 4).
        # ----------------------------------------------------------------
        if(~eu(issue_eu).valid){
            eu(issue_eu).valid := exe_valid;
            var foo : word_t;
            eu(issue_eu).res := foo;
            assume f(exe_opra,exe_oprb,eu(issue_eu).res);
            eu(issue_eu).tag(X) := exe_tag = X;
        };

        # ----------------------------------------------------------------
        # PHASE 7 -- ghost: advance the in-order machine in lockstep,
        # and state the refinement for the observable output.
        # ----------------------------------------------------------------
        if ~stallout {
            var refdout := ref.step(opin,srca,srcb,dst,din,st_choice,issue_choice);

            # [refines_read] THE OBSERVABLE REFINEMENT.  A register read that
            # is answered (i.e. does not stall) returns exactly what the
            # in-order machine would have returned.  This is the statement the
            # whole invariant set exists to support; it is an `ensure`, so
            # ivy_check discharges it as a proof obligation of the action.
            ensure opin = op_rd -> dout = refdout
        };

        if (exe_valid) {
            aux(issue_choice).eu := issue_eu;       # ghost
        };
    }


    # ####################################################################
    #  7.  THE INDUCTIVE INVARIANT
    # ####################################################################
    #
    #  Eight conjuncts.  They are jointly inductive: `ivy_check` proves that
    #  `after init` establishes them and that `step` preserves them, for every
    #  choice of the nine inputs.  Read them as a description of what a tag
    #  MEANS, going outward from the value to the architectural state.
    #
    #  The reader's mental model: a tag T is a promise to deliver the value
    #  `aux(T).res`.  Each invariant says that one holder of that promise
    #  (a register, another station's operand slot, an execution unit, the
    #  bus) has the promise recorded correctly.

    # --- (1),(2)  A captured operand is the in-order operand. ------------
    # If a station has already latched an operand value, that value is the one
    # the in-order machine read for this instruction.  This is what makes the
    # ALU computation in phase 6 compute the right thing.
    invariant [inv_opra_val]
        st(K).valid & st(K).opra.valid -> st(K).opra.val = aux(K).opra
    invariant [inv_oprb_val]
        st(K).valid & st(K).oprb.valid -> st(K).oprb.val = aux(K).oprb

    # --- (3)  An execution unit is a faithful carrier of its tag. --------
    # Everything a broadcast needs to be trustworthy:
    #   st(T).valid    the station is still there to be freed;
    #   res = aux(T).res   the value on the bus will be the promised value;
    #   aux(T).eu = E  the unit is the unique holder of T (this is what makes
    #                  "one result per tag" true, and hence why freeing the
    #                  station on a matching broadcast is correct);
    #   st(T).issued   so that the broadcast is allowed to free the station.
    invariant [inv_eu]
        eu(E).valid & eu(E).tag(T)
            -> st(T).valid & eu(E).res = aux(T).res
               & aux(T).eu = E & st(T).issued

    # --- (4)  An un-renamed register agrees with the in-order machine. ---
    # Half of the refinement statement (section 2).
    invariant [inv_reg_ready]
        ~ir(R).resvd -> ir(R).val = ref.r(R)

    # --- (5)  A renamed register's promise is already the in-order value. -
    # The other half.  Note `aux(K).res = ref.r(R)`: the in-order machine has
    # *already* executed this instruction (phase 7 runs it at dispatch), so
    # the value it will deliver is known at renaming time.  `st(K).dst = R`
    # makes the ownership exclusive: a live station owns exactly the register
    # it renamed, so two registers cannot be waiting on the same tag.
    invariant [inv_reg_reserved]
        ir(R).resvd & ir(R).tag(K)
            -> st(K).valid & aux(K).res = ref.r(R) & st(K).dst = R

    # --- (6),(7)  A waiting operand slot points at a live producer. ------
    # The wake-up graph is well formed: a slot waiting for T is waiting for a
    # station that still exists and whose eventual result is exactly the
    # operand this slot needs.  These two are the invariants that forced the
    # relational encoding of section 3 (i).
    invariant [inv_opra_dep]
        st(K).valid & ~st(K).opra.valid & st(K).opra.tag(T)
            -> st(T).valid & aux(T).res = aux(K).opra
    invariant [inv_oprb_dep]
        st(K).valid & ~st(K).oprb.valid & st(K).oprb.tag(T)
            -> st(T).valid & aux(T).res = aux(K).oprb

    # --- (8)  The ghost record is internally consistent. -----------------
    # The promised result really is the ALU applied to the promised operands.
    # This is the conjunct that connects phase 6 (which computes
    # f(opra.val,oprb.val)) to `aux(T).res` via (1),(2).
    invariant [inv_aux_alu]
        st(T).valid -> f(aux(T).opra,aux(T).oprb,aux(T).res)


    # ####################################################################
    #  7.1  DERIVED FACTS (not needed for induction, stated because they
    #       are the architecturally meaningful consequences)
    # ####################################################################

    # A tag is broadcast by at most one execution unit: renaming is injective
    # on live tags, so a value can never be delivered twice.  Follows from
    # [inv_eu] (`aux(T).eu = E`).
    invariant [derived_unique_producer]
        eu(E1).valid & eu(E2).valid & eu(E1).tag(T) & eu(E2).tag(T) -> E1 = E2

    # A tag renames at most one architectural register.  Follows from
    # [inv_reg_reserved] (`st(K).dst = R`).  This is the property that makes
    # write-after-write hazards impossible.
    invariant [derived_unique_renamed_reg]
        ir(R1).resvd & ir(R1).tag(K) & ir(R2).resvd & ir(R2).tag(K) -> R1 = R2

    # A station that is executing in a unit is still a live station: a result
    # can never come back for a station that has already been freed and
    # re-allocated.  Follows from [inv_eu].
    invariant [derived_no_orphan_result]
        eu(E).valid & eu(E).tag(T) -> st(T).valid & st(T).issued
}

export toma.step

# ############################################################################
#
#   8.  RUNNING IT, AND CHECKING IT IS NOT VACUOUS
#   ==============================================
#
#       $ ~/workplace/venv_ivy/bin/ivy_check tomasulo_documented.ivy
#       ...
#       OK
#
#   The model contains three `assume`s (the ALU relation twice, the CDB tag
#   once).  Assumptions can only remove behaviours, so they cannot make a
#   failed proof succeed unsoundly -- but they *can* make it vacuous by
#   removing all behaviours.  Three experiments, all actually run:
#
#     * DECOY 1.  Add
#
#           invariant [decoy] ~(st(T).valid & st(T).issued)
#
#       -> `toma.decoy ... FAIL`.  Stations really are allocated and issued,
#       so phases 4 and 5 are reachable.
#
#     * DECOY 2.  Add
#
#           invariant [decoy2] ~pout.valid
#
#       -> `toma.decoy2 ... FAIL`.  Results really are broadcast on the CDB,
#       so phases 1 and 3 are reachable too.  (Both decoys fail at init as
#       well, because `st.issued`, `eu.ready` and `pout` are unconstrained at
#       init; the interesting failure is the second one, "must preserve the
#       invariant".)
#
#     * THE `eu.tag` HOLE.  `eu(E).tag` is unconstrained at init, and phase 1
#       reads it through `assume eu(complete_eu).tag(pout.tag)`.  If the
#       oracle started that relation empty, every cycle with that
#       `complete_eu` would be blocked, so the invariant would never be
#       checked on those cycles.  `eu(E).tag(T) := true` in `after init`
#       removes the hole for free.  (Deleting the line does *not* make either
#       decoy stop failing -- initial values are chosen existentially when
#       `ivy_check` searches for a counterexample, so it simply picks a
#       non-empty relation.  The line is there to close the hole in the
#       *inductive* argument, not to make the model reachable.)
#
#       The direct way to close it -- stating
#
#           invariant eu(E).valid -> exists T. eu(E).tag(T)
#
#       -- is rejected:  `error: The verification condition is not in the
#       fragment FAU.`  This is section 3 biting again, and it is worth
#       understanding: the existential Skolemises to a function eu_t -> tag_t,
#       which closes a cycle with the ghost field `aux(T).eu : tag_t -> eu_t`
#       used by [inv_eu].  Exactly the forall-exists trap that LIVENESS_GUIDE
#       section 10.3 (7) warns about.  The `after init` assignment is the EPR
#       way to get the same effect.
#
#   9.  WHAT IS NOT PROVED HERE
#   ===========================
#
#   Nothing in this file says the machine ever makes progress.  It is
#   perfectly consistent with every invariant above that `complete_eu` always
#   names an idle unit, that `issue_choice` always names a station that is
#   already issued, or that no execution unit is ever `ready` -- all of which
#   are allowed because the arbiters are adversarial inputs.  Turning the
#   model into one where "every instruction that enters a reservation station
#   eventually retires" is a theorem requires (a) making the arbiters fair and
#   (b) a well-founded argument over the wake-up graph of invariants (6),(7).
#   That is `tomasulo_liveness.ivy`.
#
# ############################################################################
